1
00:00:02,096 --> 00:00:10,399
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question is how to practice as a layperson, or rather, how to make progress as a householder.

2
00:00:11,379 --> 00:00:15,541
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in your household life, how to develop your dhamma practice further.

3
00:00:16,201 --> 00:00:22,663
[SPEAKER_00]: The first point would be to recognize that... Well, we said that before, that you are responsible for your environment.

4
00:00:24,243 --> 00:00:28,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're a householder, you have to take responsibility for choosing to remain so.

5
00:00:29,722 --> 00:00:41,485
[SPEAKER_00]: And the same, like, if you choose to stop being a householder and go forth, become a monk or a nun, again, you will be responsible for your actions and consequences that would come out of it.

6
00:00:41,545 --> 00:00:56,390
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason why people would try and avoid responsibility for that, for whatever they are, for things they chose to do and now they have to live with the results of those things, is because it would make you feel guilty, so to speak.

7
00:00:56,450 --> 00:00:56,650
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

8
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:06,466
[SPEAKER_00]: If you, for example, you value practice of the dhamma, you want to progress in the dhamma, but you have to look after your family now.

9
00:01:06,646 --> 00:01:08,948
[SPEAKER_00]: That family hasn't been sort of thrown upon you.

10
00:01:09,508 --> 00:01:17,514
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a result of your actions, of your choices before, maybe before you came across the dhamma, but nevertheless, you are responsible for it.

11
00:01:17,634 --> 00:01:25,839
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if now you would like to not have to look after other people, because you want to dedicate your life to practice in the dhamma, you can't, because

12
00:01:26,879 --> 00:01:33,261
[SPEAKER_00]: You've made such actions, you've done such actions and choices that have resulted in other people being dependent upon you.

13
00:01:35,042 --> 00:01:46,386
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to recognize that you are responsible for the environment and that means that you cannot use environment as an excuse.

14
00:01:47,586 --> 00:01:48,626
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is a difference there.

15
00:01:48,646 --> 00:01:53,148
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a difference between you accepting responsibility

16
00:01:55,467 --> 00:02:01,491
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... not excusing yourself for not practicing further because of the environment.

17
00:02:02,232 --> 00:02:20,825
[SPEAKER_00]: But more often than not, what people do is they, eh... they don't accept the responsibility in a sense of... they might accept the responsibility on the surface, but underneath they, eh... they still regard and have the pretty much a view that the environment excuses you from not practicing further.

18
00:02:21,065 --> 00:02:22,326
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's... that's not the case.

19
00:02:22,987 --> 00:02:24,027
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you choose then

20
00:02:25,308 --> 00:02:31,271
[SPEAKER_00]: environment to become a reason, a justification for your lack of further practice.

21
00:02:31,611 --> 00:02:33,652
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even as a householder, it doesn't matter.

22
00:02:34,853 --> 00:02:46,419
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is to accept that responsibility and that means not to use your environment as an excuse for your lack of development, for, you know, for still being a putujana, for example, not being free from suffering.

23
00:02:47,419 --> 00:02:52,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to, you want to, you want to kind of remove that attitude

24
00:02:53,980 --> 00:03:00,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it does... it just removes any basis for correct practice, for as long as you're covering it up.

25
00:03:01,103 --> 00:03:17,609
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you factually recognize that other people's lives might be compromised if you stop looking after them, be it your children, your spouses, your parents, but

26
00:03:18,358 --> 00:03:23,282
[SPEAKER_00]: At the same time, you actually do not use that as an excuse for not practicing.

27
00:03:24,202 --> 00:03:33,389
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that will kind of force you to... to make... well, force you into a maximum effort within that compromised environment.

28
00:03:35,510 --> 00:03:40,514
[SPEAKER_00]: And this ties in with my next point, which is a sense restraint, precepts and even celibacy.

29
00:03:42,415 --> 00:03:43,456
[SPEAKER_00]: Often, people would

30
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:48,402
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, you don't have to ordain and become a monk

31
00:04:14,265 --> 00:04:21,589
[SPEAKER_00]: But that is not to say that you can also not practice sensory strain and develop in dhamma.

32
00:04:23,450 --> 00:04:34,717
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in simple terms, people find a certain factual basis of needing to stay a layman or a laywoman, but then the factual basis

33
00:04:36,295 --> 00:04:43,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not taken responsibly, in the sense we just described, that factual basis then becomes an excuse, and as such, it becomes a blanket excuse.

34
00:04:44,597 --> 00:04:57,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Blanket excuse for not accepting... sorry, blanket excuse for not going... not going forth, but then also blanket excuse for now, because I'm not going forth, I don't need to practice sensory strain here either.

35
00:04:58,161 --> 00:04:59,821
[SPEAKER_00]: And as I said, those are two separate things.

36
00:05:00,361 --> 00:05:02,982
[SPEAKER_00]: You can... you can stay a layman for the rest of your life,

37
00:05:04,091 --> 00:05:04,871
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's one thing.

38
00:05:05,171 --> 00:05:16,997
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you choose that being a layman means I don't have to be sensory-strained and practice the dhamma, that's just a contradiction in terms of on all sorts of levels.

39
00:05:17,077 --> 00:05:17,297
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

40
00:05:19,143 --> 00:05:23,725
[SPEAKER_00]: And by the way, this applies to those who are interested in actually practicing the dhamma.

41
00:05:24,025 --> 00:05:36,751
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like practicing dhamma because it makes me feel good or helps me with things, but practicing the dhamma in the form of... in the way that the Buddha was teaching the suttas, which is basically undoing your being, undoing the bhava, undoing your sense of self.

42
00:05:37,552 --> 00:05:42,634
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not a... it's not something you can do on the weekends only or on a ten-day retreat.

43
00:05:43,354 --> 00:05:45,515
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your whole life needs to start revolving around it.

44
00:05:45,736 --> 00:05:48,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter if that life is in a household setting or not.

45
00:05:50,265 --> 00:06:17,913
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to... you have to recognize that... that there is a... there is a certain... there's a certain kind of... well, kind of a natural tendency, tendency of an ignorant mind, to lean towards finding that excuse that will remove that sense of guilt or pressure that you might feel upon you, when you recognize that certain values, certain things that you value yourself, are being contradicted by your very lifestyle and your very actions.

46
00:06:19,171 --> 00:06:28,715
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it seems to be an automatic thing whereby people would think that if I'm not a monk, I don't need to be sensory-strained.

47
00:06:29,615 --> 00:06:30,496
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's just silly.

48
00:06:31,616 --> 00:06:38,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the only time that would be an accurate thing to say, oh, I'm not a monk, I don't need to be sensory-strained, is if you don't want to practice dharma.

49
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:41,800
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, yeah, you don't need to be sensory-strained.

50
00:06:41,820 --> 00:06:42,761
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to do anything.

51
00:06:43,761 --> 00:06:46,062
[SPEAKER_00]: But the slightest familiarity with the suttas

52
00:06:47,153 --> 00:06:55,978
[SPEAKER_00]: We'll just show what a silly view this is, because if you want to practice the dhamma, there is no dhamma without a basis of sīla and sensory strength.

53
00:06:56,818 --> 00:07:05,023
[SPEAKER_00]: How good, how well established that basis is, that's how well the dhamma will grow, or not.

54
00:07:06,005 --> 00:07:10,427
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the case, by the way, for the monks and lay people.

55
00:07:10,447 --> 00:07:11,987
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no difference there, in that sense.

56
00:07:12,527 --> 00:07:26,813
[SPEAKER_00]: And even those... the arahants, after the Buddha's passing away, when they met in the first council, when they were deciding what should they recite first, the suttas, dhamma suttas or vinaya suttas, the origin stories of the rules and precepts,

57
00:07:27,753 --> 00:07:39,303
[SPEAKER_00]: They've unanimously decided to recite the rules first, the origin story of how every rule came to be, because they said there is no dhamma without sīla.

58
00:07:39,904 --> 00:07:43,727
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, sīla is not the dhamma, but there is no dhamma without the sīla.

59
00:07:45,989 --> 00:07:53,676
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you think you can practice the dhamma without a sense restraint, literally you're saying, ah, you found the way to practice the dhamma without sīla.

60
00:07:54,835 --> 00:08:00,717
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's something even Buddha himself could not have found, because there isn't one.

61
00:08:01,437 --> 00:08:07,460
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, how much you will progress in your lay setting will depend how well you're sense-restrained.

62
00:08:08,060 --> 00:08:19,544
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can have duties and responsibilities and work, and all of that will be putting, you know, stress on you and everything else, but none of that can be a reason for being unrestrained.

63
00:08:20,820 --> 00:08:32,931
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how well you develop that restraint in that adverse environment of constant distraction, pressure, demands of others, from others and so on, that's how well you will progress.

64
00:08:33,071 --> 00:08:36,454
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you would... people would go forth and change the environment.

65
00:08:36,474 --> 00:08:44,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because somehow, magically, by putting on robes, sense restraint, you just... becomes easy and you go... and you're just flying effortlessly.

66
00:08:45,042 --> 00:08:52,469
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's just simply because the environment is more suitable for sense restraint, because you wouldn't have to deal with so many things distracting you all the time.

67
00:08:53,570 --> 00:09:01,397
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm saying, if there is a reason that you cannot do that, that's still... the principle of the practice does not change.

68
00:09:01,657 --> 00:09:03,839
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't care for your reasons, basically.

69
00:09:04,580 --> 00:09:09,204
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have a reason for... that prevents you from going forth and... and ordaining,

70
00:09:10,121 --> 00:09:17,704
[SPEAKER_00]: That's fine, but that reason cannot become your justification now for lack of sense-restraint, for lack of celibacy, and so on.

71
00:09:18,665 --> 00:09:23,827
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how far you want to progress in practice is how far you want to be restrained.

72
00:09:24,227 --> 00:09:29,369
[SPEAKER_00]: I think Ajahn Chah said something like, if you give up little, you will find little peace.

73
00:09:29,429 --> 00:09:30,910
[SPEAKER_00]: If you give up more, you find more peace.

74
00:09:30,970 --> 00:09:34,731
[SPEAKER_00]: If you give everything up, you'll find the ultimate peace.

75
00:09:35,732 --> 00:09:38,493
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nobody is excused from that principle.

76
00:09:40,235 --> 00:10:01,211
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want to, you know, have a mediocre progress in dhamma, so to speak, have some sense of a greater spiritual purpose, they will not require you to give things up significantly and it will not require you to experience the pain of sense restraint, sometimes for days in that lay setting.

77
00:10:02,534 --> 00:10:06,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, I guess, five precepts would be enough.

78
00:10:06,897 --> 00:10:10,939
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't really regard it as a significant progress in the dhamma.

79
00:10:11,759 --> 00:10:21,905
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, back in the day, when the Buddha... Like, first of all, they haven't really, for most of their life, they haven't even taught the dhamma to lay people, because it would make them give up their lay life.

80
00:10:22,758 --> 00:10:32,741
[SPEAKER_00]: And it was only when Anantapindika, on his deathbed, heard Sariputta giving the teaching, then made himself paññā, he said, oh, why didn't you... why didn't you teach this to lay people before?

81
00:10:34,302 --> 00:10:38,463
[SPEAKER_00]: And then begged him to do so, because there are those people who will be able to understand.

82
00:10:39,383 --> 00:10:48,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so vast majority won't, because vast majority will find it too unpleasant to withdraw themselves from sensuality while they live in the central world, but there are those who can do it.

83
00:10:49,427 --> 00:10:49,627
[SPEAKER_00]: And...

84
00:10:51,145 --> 00:10:56,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, even those people who understood the dhamma and practiced, they've done so because...

85
00:10:57,450 --> 00:11:00,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Their lifestyle was already being aligned with it, and they were making effort.

86
00:11:00,411 --> 00:11:12,494
[SPEAKER_00]: They have not have... people have not magically become anāgāmīs in the suttas, as householders, and then started practicing sense-restraint, which seems to be what people expect nowadays.

87
00:11:12,514 --> 00:11:18,836
[SPEAKER_00]: No, they've been sense-restrained, they've been virtuous, they've been generous, they've been obviously contemplating dhamma rightly.

88
00:11:19,376 --> 00:11:25,658
[SPEAKER_00]: As a result of it, they have become free from sensuality completely, developed jhānas completely, and so on.

89
00:11:27,106 --> 00:11:30,267
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's important to accept that responsibility.

90
00:11:31,108 --> 00:11:42,012
[SPEAKER_00]: They will prevent you from using your situation as an excuse, and they will basically outline exactly how much you can progress, because you will know how much you can be sense-restrained.

91
00:11:42,633 --> 00:11:48,615
[SPEAKER_00]: And to that extent, you will create a basis for the dhamma to apply to.

92
00:11:48,855 --> 00:11:54,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can know all the suttas and everything, but if there is no basis of virtue, as those arahants said,

93
00:11:55,600 --> 00:12:00,643
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that will have any fertile ground to grow from.

94
00:12:00,723 --> 00:12:16,391
[SPEAKER_00]: So, consequently, if you have a well, thoroughly, high-quality established virtue, and very little knowledge of the dhamma, it will still grow, because the fertile ground is there, and it's very potent, so to speak.

95
00:12:20,654 --> 00:12:21,634
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it comes down to that.

96
00:12:25,018 --> 00:12:39,792
[SPEAKER_00]: How willing you are to take the pains of sense-restraint, and if you if you want to make a significant strides towards freedom from suffering, yeah, you have to aim even for for celibacy, even as a household, like the ones in the suttas.

97
00:12:40,212 --> 00:12:44,756
[SPEAKER_00]: If back then sense-restraint was necessary, you can only imagine how much more so it is today.

98
00:12:46,017 --> 00:12:48,420
[SPEAKER_00]: It's simply not optional.

99
00:12:49,020 --> 00:12:49,120
[SPEAKER_00]: And

100
00:12:51,707 --> 00:13:05,188
[SPEAKER_00]: And the aim should be also, for that sense restraint, as like a lifetime goal, not something I'll do over the weekend and hopefully I'll understand the dhamma over the weekend and then I can go back to my lay unrestrained ways.

101
00:13:06,130 --> 00:13:07,732
[SPEAKER_00]: It needs to be taken on as a value.

102
00:13:08,532 --> 00:13:20,062
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, even if you sometimes might slip back, break the precepts or lose sense restraint, the value remains there as something that will pull you back towards the right direction.

103
00:13:20,522 --> 00:13:26,748
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't value it in that ultimate sense, as something to be ultimately developed and maintained,

104
00:13:27,937 --> 00:13:32,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you're not... you will not be restrained even when you are, so to speak.

105
00:13:33,226 --> 00:13:37,112
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, for example, when people try to give up an addiction, say, smoking.

106
00:13:39,409 --> 00:13:44,611
[SPEAKER_00]: It's much harder to give it up if your decision to give up pertains for the rest of your life.

107
00:13:44,931 --> 00:13:54,934
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you say, I will never smoke again for the rest of my life, you will feel the weight of that much more than if you just try and give up smoking.

108
00:13:55,014 --> 00:13:56,595
[SPEAKER_00]: And, well, let me see how I feel.

109
00:13:56,675 --> 00:13:57,895
[SPEAKER_00]: I won't smoke for a while.

110
00:13:58,295 --> 00:14:02,257
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, sometimes people can go for months without cigarettes, but they never really stop smoking.

111
00:14:02,777 --> 00:14:05,258
[SPEAKER_00]: And they will always have to go back to it.

112
00:14:05,658 --> 00:14:05,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

113
00:14:07,193 --> 00:14:13,275
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way to not go back to it is to have aimed at that ultimate value that you will never smoke again.

114
00:14:13,676 --> 00:14:17,977
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, even if you fail and go back to it, that ultimate value will keep you in the right direction.

115
00:14:18,958 --> 00:14:23,840
[SPEAKER_00]: And in that sense, that guilt that I was talking about in the beginning of this video is useful.

116
00:14:24,900 --> 00:14:28,662
[SPEAKER_00]: It will realign you with that which you value, which is why you feel guilty.

117
00:14:29,387 --> 00:14:36,731
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the worst thing is to... to try and justify it and cover it up and psychologize your way out of it, so you don't feel discomfort.

118
00:14:36,791 --> 00:14:38,572
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a good type of discomfort.

119
00:14:39,552 --> 00:14:43,954
[SPEAKER_00]: Like guilt on account of losing the virtue, losing the sensory strain, is a good guilt.

120
00:14:44,395 --> 00:14:45,155
[SPEAKER_00]: It's what the Buddha said.

121
00:14:45,175 --> 00:14:48,917
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what protects the world from... from doing immoral acts.

122
00:14:49,417 --> 00:14:50,638
[SPEAKER_00]: You will feel unpleasant.

123
00:14:50,778 --> 00:14:52,219
[SPEAKER_00]: You will feel ashamed.

124
00:14:53,079 --> 00:14:58,662
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... you will have a sense of, like, fear, because what you value, you went against it.

125
00:14:59,542 --> 00:15:00,202
[SPEAKER_00]: And not just that.

126
00:15:00,522 --> 00:15:09,424
[SPEAKER_00]: If you practice like that, you will also be be able to to see these things, unwholesome things, even before you commit to them, before you act out of them.

127
00:15:09,444 --> 00:15:15,706
[SPEAKER_00]: The the choice of either choosing wholesome or unwholesome would be much more clearly outlined for you.

128
00:15:17,326 --> 00:15:27,429
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how to progress as a lay person in the dhamma is really... comes down to deciding how far you want to progress in that practice.

129
00:15:29,231 --> 00:15:41,485
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're clear how far you want to go, then it will be clear to you how far you should be restrained and how far you need to make that effort of enduring the discomfort of a household environment.

130
00:15:42,126 --> 00:15:47,893
[SPEAKER_00]: And it will also then make your efforts towards dhamma contemplation and study much more efficient.

131
00:15:48,648 --> 00:15:50,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, for example, the example I gave.

132
00:15:50,889 --> 00:15:56,210
[SPEAKER_00]: People naturally think that avoiding an overcoming sense of guilt is good.

133
00:15:57,010 --> 00:16:00,691
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as you see yourself just now, it's not always the case.

134
00:16:01,031 --> 00:16:07,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sometimes you might be, rightfully so, feeling guilty on account of something you've done.

135
00:16:08,953 --> 00:16:10,433
[SPEAKER_00]: And you should not cover that up.

136
00:16:11,454 --> 00:16:16,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Endure that pain, that remorse, because it is reshaping the value then.

137
00:16:17,413 --> 00:16:18,534
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to the thing you've done.

138
00:16:18,574 --> 00:16:20,575
[SPEAKER_00]: So, next time, you won't make the same mistake.

139
00:16:21,315 --> 00:16:23,137
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you force yourself to learn from it.

140
00:16:24,597 --> 00:16:26,799
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's really the... the unpleasant thing.

141
00:16:27,399 --> 00:16:31,141
[SPEAKER_00]: People think you can... you can learn the dhamma and practice dhamma on your own terms.

142
00:16:32,362 --> 00:16:32,942
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't.

143
00:16:33,643 --> 00:16:39,266
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way to do it rightly is to set yourself up for being towards that practice.

144
00:16:39,466 --> 00:16:45,510
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, set yourself up to prevent yourself from being... from choosing the non-practice.

145
00:16:46,070 --> 00:16:46,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's put it like that.

146
00:16:48,001 --> 00:17:05,638
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through practicing sensory strength, to acknowledging the guilt of wrongdoing, of losing that sensory strength, to not using environment as an excuse, you set yourself up towards facing basically that which needs to be dealt with, which is avoiding the pain, avoiding the suffering.

147
00:17:06,338 --> 00:17:07,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Avoiding the discomfort.

148
00:17:07,640 --> 00:17:09,922
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's always the root of the entire sensual world.

149
00:17:10,202 --> 00:17:15,648
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason people go back to sense desires, or pursue the sense desires, is because it's too unpleasant to not do so.

150
00:17:16,228 --> 00:17:21,293
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where people would then develop the view and try to justify that, no, you don't need to be sense-restrained, and you can practice dhamma.

151
00:17:21,373 --> 00:17:22,595
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all of that.

152
00:17:23,215 --> 00:17:30,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Even say... even if they were theoretically true, it's not true individually, because individually it's always rooted in trying to avoid pain.

153
00:17:31,242 --> 00:17:35,586
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what you're saying is, yes, you can free yourself from pain by trying to avoid pain.

154
00:17:36,066 --> 00:17:39,429
[SPEAKER_00]: By pursuing distraction from pain, you will find a way out of pain.

155
00:17:40,030 --> 00:17:42,151
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a complete contradiction in terms.

156
00:17:43,272 --> 00:17:44,493
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're willing...

157
00:17:44,513 --> 00:17:47,196
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if... as I said, if you... if you... if you sort of, um...

158
00:17:49,247 --> 00:18:01,037
